\description{
\code{logit.debt()} calibrates consumer demand using Logit and then simulates
the price effect of a merger between two firms under the assumption that
all firms in the market are playing a differentiated-products Bertrand
pricing game with firm-level debt. Limited liability enters via a
default-threshold outside share (see \code{design/ARCHITECTURE.md}).
}
\details{
This is a \strong{parity migration} of \code{MergerDebt::logit.debt()} (SHA
\code{ea727430}, \code{R/DebtFunctions.R}): the legacy function name, argument
order, and default values are preserved exactly so that existing calling
code and the golden-master oracle fixtures continue to apply unchanged.
The only behavioral changes are the documented legacy-defect fixes below;
none of them alter the economics for any of the golden-master fixture
inputs (verified in \code{tests/testthat/test-debt-parity.R}).

Let k denote the number of products produced by all firms across all
markets. Let f denote the number of firms producing products across all
markets. Let m denote the number of markets.

Using product prices, quantity shares, and finite margins for every
product, \code{logit.debt} recovers the price
coefficient and product mean valuations in a Logit demand model, subject
to a firm-level debt/limited-liability default threshold. \code{logit.debt}
then uses these calibrated parameters to simulate a merger between two
firms.

Legacy defects fixed during migration (see \code{design/ARCHITECTURE.md}'s
"Known legacy defects to fix during migration"):
\itemize{
\item \code{numDeriv::genD} is now explicitly namespace-qualified in
\code{calcPrices(LogitDebt, isMax = TRUE)} (previously an unqualified
\code{genD()} call that only worked if \code{numDeriv} happened to be
attached).
\item The historical two-market documentation example required \code{mktSize <- c(1, 1)} (the legacy doc example set
\code{mktSize <- 1}, which fails \code{LogitDebt}'s own validity check that
\code{length(mktSize) == nMarkets}).
\item The \code{mktSize} default itself is fixed from
\code{rep(1, unique(marketID))} to \code{rep(1, length(unique(marketID)))}
(see the \code{mktSize} parameter documentation above).
\item The \code{density} prototype/argument default mismatch is resolved:
\code{LogitDebt}'s class prototype now defaults to \code{"beta"}, matching this
constructor's default, instead of the legacy prototype's \code{"uniform"}.
\item The \code{Bertran} typo in the legacy roxygen \verb{@section Extends:} docs
is corrected to \code{Bertrand} (see \code{LogitDebt-class}).
}

None of these fixes changes the calibrated/simulated economics for any
